\title{Artifact attachments}
\import{base-macros}
\tag{feature-demo}
\tag{showcase}
\meta{venue}{Feature gallery}
\meta{artifact-file:drafts/first-sketch.svg}{\route-asset{assets/feat-artifacts/sketch.svg}}
\meta{artifact-note:drafts/first-sketch.svg}{The original whiteboard idea, before any formalization.}
\meta{artifact-file:drafts/outline.md}{\route-asset{assets/feat-artifacts/outline.md}}

\p{A tree can carry its \em{proto} origins the rough sketches, scans, or files a
note grew out of as explicitly accessible artifacts. They are declared in
frontmatter and surface in a subtle, collapsible \strong{Attachments} panel at
the foot of the tree: a little file-tree with per-file download buttons,
optional explanatory notes, and folder grouping. Nothing is inlined into the
prose; the artifacts sit set apart, available but out of the way.}

\p{This very note began as a rough whiteboard
sketch\link{#artifact:drafts/first-sketch.svg}{↗} that later became the draft
outline beside it the marker jumps to the artifact the same way a citation
jumps to its footnote. Both files live under a \code{drafts} folder in the
panel below, which is rendered live from this tree's own frontmatter.}

\p{Declaring an artifact takes one \code{meta} line pointing at a
\code{route-asset}; the key after \code{artifact-file:} is the display path
(its folders drive the tree view, its basename is the download filename), and
an optional \code{artifact-note:} on the same key adds the explainer. Reference
one inline with \code{link} to a \code{#artifact:} fragment:}

\codeblock{text}{\startverb
\meta{artifact-file:drafts/first-sketch.svg}{\route-asset{assets/feat-artifacts/sketch.svg}}
\meta{artifact-note:drafts/first-sketch.svg}{The original whiteboard idea.}

In prose: ...began as a rough sketch \link{#artifact:drafts/first-sketch.svg}{↗} ...
\stopverb}

\p{In the local dev server you rarely write these by hand: paste an image with
\code{CTRL+V}, confirm the popover, and the declaration is injected into this
tree's frontmatter for you.}
